A visitor already signed in to Volto is no longer asked to sign in again at the authorization endpoint. `@@oauth-authorize` is a browser view, so it needs a Zope principal; Volto keeps its `jwt_auth` token in an `auth_token` cookie and sends it as an `Authorization` header only on its own API calls, and `plone.restapi`'s JWT plugin reads only that header. A top-level navigation carried the cookie and no header, so the visitor was anonymous and was challenged by a site they were already signed in to.

The `[server]` layer now installs a PAS plugin that reads that cookie — for the authorization endpoint and nothing else. `plone.restapi` declines to read it for a good reason: a bearer credential the browser attaches on its own turns every browser view into a CSRF target. The scope check runs before the cookie is read, so every other view in the site behaves exactly as `plone.restapi` left it, and an `Authorization` header always outranks the cookie. What remains exposed is one GET that renders a consent screen, whose decision is a POST carrying a form token a forged submission does not have.

Decoding is `plone.restapi`'s own, so the key, the algorithm, the expiry and any configured token store apply unchanged: this plugin decides when the site's session cookie may be read, never whether the token in it is valid. @ericof
